AB  - Riverbank erosion and deposition continually reshape channel morphology and affect riparian stability along the Mekong River in Loei Province, Thailand, which is one of the most dynamic reaches of the Thai Lao border. This study develops an integrated Remote Sensing and Machine Learning framework to quantify and predict riverbank change from 1988 to 2025 using multi temporal Landsat imagery processed through Google Earth Engine. Annual dry season composites were used to generate water masks based on the Modified Normalized Difference Water Index for mapping both erosion and deposition. Six spectral indices, together with rainfall and water surface area, were used as predictor variables for Random Forest and Support Vector Regression models. Results show that Random Forest achieved substantially better performance for both deposition and erosion, indicating a stronger ability to represent nonlinear and long-term geomorphic variability. Turbidity related indices were identified as the most influential predictors. Persistent high change zones were found near Pak Chom and Chiang Khan, demonstrating the value of integrating satellite-based indicators with machine learning for long term riverbank monitoring and risk assessment along the Mekong River.
