AB  - Leaf Area Index (LAI) is a crucial vegetation parameter that determines the efficiency of sunlight absorption and utilization by leaves. It is closely related to the biological and physical processes of vegetation. The acquisition methods for LAI include ground measurement and remote sensing inversion. Remote sensing inversion, as a significant means for large-scale monitoring, holds great significance for the evaluation of global LAI remote sensing products. In this study, we selected the Yucheng Farmland Ecological Station in Shandong Province and utilized Sentinel-2 satellite data to retrieve LAI. When implementing the inversion process with the PROSAIL model as the theoretical basis, the traditional algorithmic approach generally requires iterative estimations of multiple biophysical parameters. This relatively complex parameter retrieval mechanism may result in suboptimal computational performance and a relatively high demand for computing resources. Therefore, we constructed a Sentinel-2 LAI batch inversion algorithm centered on the Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) hybrid model on the Google Earth Engine (GEE) platform. This algorithm initially simulates Sentinel-2 L1C-level data using the PROSAIL model and processes it with the 6SV model. Subsequently, the GPR model is optimized and trained using active learning technology. Finally, batch inversion is achieved through steps such as image processing and data extraction. By comparing the ground-measured LAI data with the inversion results and evaluating them using the R2, MAE, RMSE, and EE, the results indicate that the Sentinel-2 LAI inverted from March to December 2020 shows good consistency and periodicity with the ground-measured values. The R2, value reached 0.805, the RMSE was 0.55, and the MAE was 0.46. Compared with the inversion results of the PROSAIL model, the difference values between the two LAI pixel maps are concentrated between -0.092 and 0.165. This suggests that the batch inversion algorithm can efficiently and accurately obtain LAI data and has high consistency with traditional methods, providing powerful support for vegetation research.
