AB  - In recent years, deep learning (DL) has emerged as one of the most widely utilized techniques in hyperspectral anomaly detection (HAD) with an impressive detection accuracy. However, the investigation into the diverse backgrounds (BKs) representation and the spatialspectral interaction remains underexplored. To tackle this, we propose a novel framework namely the prototype-guided and spatialspectral interaction network (PSSIN) for HAD in this article. Specifically, an adaptive anomaly mask module (AAMM) is utilized to mitigate the interference of the BK reconstruction caused by the blending of potential anomalies. Subsequently, we design a BK-guided prototype autoencoder (BP-AE) to represent the BKs with various land cover types, incorporating two critical components: the BK prototype module (BPM) and the spatialspectral interaction block (SSIB). To characterize different typical BK features by a global perspective, BPM utilizes a prototype learning strategy with a self-attention mechanism, and a multivariate ensemble loss is employed for BPM to optimize the transformation of BK features and the updating of a prototype codebook. To enhance the spatialspectral utilization of window-based approach, SSIB first introduces a spatialspectral interaction paradigm for HAD. The window-based self-attention branch is to mine spatial feature characteristics, while the depth-wise convolution branch is to extract spectral features. These two branches in a parallel configuration interact with each other’s features and then perform feature fusion. SSIB architecture not only broadens the receptive fields by concurrently modeling the intrawindow and cross-window relationships but also facilitates bi-directional interactions between the spatial and spectral branches. Furthermore, the comprehensive experiments conducted on six authentic datasets have fully validated its superior performance.
