AB  - Accurate classification of soil carbon stock is vital for global climate stability and supports climate change mitigation. Traditional field-based methods are often limited in scalability and efficiency, encouraging the use of remote sensing and machine learning. While destructive sampling is considered the most accurate for estimating tree biomass, it requires special permits that are often hard to obtain. This study aims to develop an accurate method for classifying soil carbon stock using a hybrid approach that combines ResNet50 for feature extraction with CatBoost and Random Forest for classification. Three datasets were used: 2,114 drone images, 2,526 satellite images from Google Earth Engine (GEE), and a combined set of 2,320 mixed images. Field data were collected from carbon plots at Telkom University and classified into low, medium, and high carbon stock levels. All images were resized, preprocessed, and passed through ResNet50 to extract high-level features before classification. Model performance was evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. The results show that drone imagery outperformed GEE data. CatBoost achieved the highest test accuracy (84.19%), macro precision (83.63%), recall (84.22%), and F1-score (83.44%). On the other hand, the model trained with only GEE data performed lower recall and F1-scores, hence its low capacity to identify all classes. In general, this research stresses the value of high-resolution imagery in machine learning to enhance the precision of carbon stock classification.
