AB  - The reconstruction of visible and near-infrared (VIS-NIR) spectra from satellite images is an emerging field driven by artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms designed to extract spectral information from diverse regions of interest. Several studies have demonstrated the effectiveness of computational models in analyzing optical data from the Sentinel-2 satellite of the Copernicus program, which is equipped with multispectral sensors covering 12 spectral bands (442 nm to 2200 nm) at a spatial resolution of up to 100 m2 per pixel. Accurate spectral signature identification in biodiverse regions is crucial for applications such as agricultural land detection, natural reserve management, illegal mining monitoring, water resource conservation, and sustainable energy promotion. In this work, an Al algorithm based on an autoencoder (AE) was developed to reconstruct satellite imagery from Sentinel-2 to improve ground sample spectra. The computational phase involved simulating satellite measurements using continuous spectral data, generating 12 spectral bands aligned with the precision of Sentinel-2 sensors. The goal is to enhance spectral resolution through insitu measurements, where hyperspectral images serve as both training data and reference for the algorithm, with satellite images as inputs. The neural network, trained on this dataset, reconstructs the original spectra with an average root mean square error (RMSE) of 1.2%. In the experimental phase, hyperspectral data were acquired using a SPECIM IQ camera with a spectral resolution of 204 bands ranging from 400 nm to 1000 nm. Several zones in Bogotá, Colombia, were selected to enrich the experimental dataset. Non-georeferenced colorimetry samples, varying in shape and color, were also used to enhance the neural network's ability to distinguish spectral features. The results demonstrate an adequate correlation between the satellite data and the AE reconstructions, with RMSE values below 20% and a Pearson correlation coefficient greater than 0.9.
