AB  - Coal fires from mining activities cause severe economic, environmental, and safety repercussions, making identification of spontaneous combustion areas essential for monitoring. However, the geochemical methods currently available are not appropriate for widespread fire monitoring given their high level of expense and extent of technological restrictions. In this letter, we produced coal fire data sets consisting of training, validation and test sets from thermal infrared images. We developed an accurate detection model using an image segmentation deep learning method and validated it with ground truthed data. We identified the range and count of coal fires in five representative mining areas in Wuhai, China. The trained model achieved an average precision of 93.52%, recall of 90.83%, pixel accuracy of 95.21%, and F1-score of 92.16% on the validation sets. The intersection over union ratio between detected coal fire areas and those measured in three regions with complex backgrounds was 0.95. The study identified a 0.132 km2 area with coal fires across five major mines, which had as many as 8,442 ignition points. The developed detection model has demonstrated reliability and accuracy in its ability to detect coal fires, as well as its suitability for detecting areas affected by coal fires at larger scales.
