AB  - Urban Heat Island (UHI) is a phenomenon where inner-city areas experience higher land surface temperatures (LST) compared to surrounding regions due to urbanization, land cover change, and energy use. This study develops a monthly LST forecasting model for Jakarta (2020-2024) using the ARIMA algorithm within the CRISP-DM framework. MODIS satellite data, air quality indicators, and government statistics were integrated into a structured predictive pipeline. Scenario-based simulations were conducted to assess the impact of increasing vegetation cover (+10%), reducing air pollutants (-20%), and enhancing humidity (+10%) on UHI mitigation. Results show a warming trend in Jakarta, while simulations demonstrate that vegetation expansion yields the most consistent long-term effect, with the combined intervention reducing projected LST by up to 1.9 °C. The findings emphasize the importance of evidence-based environmental planning to support low-carbon and climate-resilient urban strategies. While limited by the short five-year dataset and illustrative scenarios, this work highlights the value of integrating time-series forecasting with geospatial indicators and identifies future directions including extended timeframes and advanced machine learning models.
