AB  - Inland surface water, a crucial source of freshwater, has experienced significant changes due to climate change and human interventions, underscoring the need for effective detection and monitoring over time. During the past few decades, satellite remote sensing technology has significantly advanced, ushering in a new age for the observation of surface water. Through the appropriate application of water extraction algorithms on satellite images, both quantitative and qualitative alterations in inland water bodies can be analyzed. This review explores the satellite detection of inland surface waters, focusing on: (1) commonly used satellite sensors, including optical and microwave sensors; (2) state-of-the-art water extraction techniques, covering both conventional classification methods and machine learning approaches; and (3) several related challenges and solutions in the field, including the trade-off between spatial and temporal resolutions, monitoring in conditions of contamination and obscured by vegetation, and the water detection in a new era of remote sensing big data. This review aims to serve as a guide and resource for researchers, practitioners, and policymakers in leveraging remote sensing data for the exploration and innovative monitoring of inland surface water.
ER  -

