AB  - A wide range of high-level biophysical and geophysical products is routinely generated from satellite observations, each with distinct qualities and uncertainties. Integrating multiple satellite-derived products for the same land surface variable can effectively reduce uncertainty and enhance reliability. This chapter provides a comprehensive review of established statistical integration techniques—including optimal interpolation, empirical orthogonal functions (EOF), the multiresolution tree (MRT) method, and Bayesian model averaging (BMA)—as applied to variables such as leaf area index (LAI), broadband albedo, fraction of absorbed photosynthetically active radiation (FAPAR), land surface emissivity, longwave downward radiation, and surface latent heat flux. In addition, the chapter explores recent advances in machine learning and AI foundation models for satellite data fusion, highlighting their potential to leverage large, heterogeneous datasets, improve spatial and temporal consistency, and enable generalized, scalable integration frameworks. Key challenges and future opportunities are discussed, emphasizing the importance of ground-truth data, method selection, and the transformative potential of advanced AI-driven approaches for land surface monitoring.
