AB  - The Mu Us Sandyland, located at the core of the Yellow River's Ji-shaped bend, is a complex region characterized by the interplay of ecological restoration, coal mining, and other human activities. Groundwater serves as an essential water source for vegetation in this area, and changes in groundwater storage (GWS) significantly impact ecosystem stability and sustainable development. This study integrated multi-source data from satellite remote sensing, ground observations, and model products and employed machine learning methods to construct a monthly GWS dataset for the Mu Us Sandyland from 2003 to 2022 at a 0.05°×0.05° spatial resolution. Subsequently, trend analysis and a normalized multivariate linear regression model were applied to reveal the spatiotemporal evolution characteristics of GWS and identify its primary driving factors. The results indicate that over the 2003~2022 period, GWS in the Mu Us Sandyland declined at a rate of 9.1 mm/a, with the most pronounced depletion (15.4 mm/a) occurring in the southeastern core of the Grain-for-Green Program area. Vegetation restoration was the dominant factor driving GWS depletion, affecting 92.9% of the study area, particularly in the central and eastern regions, where large-scale ecological projects were implemented. Precipitation variability accounted for only 6.6% of the influence, mainly concentrated in the arid western zone. Thus, while large-scale ecological restoration efforts have enhanced vegetation coverage, they have also significantly increased transpiration water consumption, becoming a key driver of GWS decline. Future vegetation initiatives should prioritize water-resilient native species with low water demand, adopting a “suitable shrubs for shrubs, suitable grass for grass” approach to ensure the synergistic and sustainable development of groundwater and vegetation ecosystems in the Mu Us Sandyland.
