AB  - The revolution in remote sensing technology has significantly expanded the capacity to collect extensive geographical data, highlighting the urgent need for effective techniques to extract valuable insights from these images. A distinctive deep learning-based method is introduced in this study for transforming remote sensing images into textual descriptions. This approach leverages the capabilities of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to deliver clear and informative textual descriptions, as well as to extract features from the images. A unique collection comprising a diverse set of text-annotated remote-sensing images from various sensors and platforms has been curated for this study. The model is trained and tested using this dataset. To enhance the quality of generated descriptions, a novel attention method is introduced, facilitating the model's focus on specific regions of interest within the images. Moreover, domain-specific knowledge and spatial context are seamlessly integrated to augment the applicability and precision of textual descriptions. Extensive studies are conducted to evaluate the model's performance, including comparisons with alternative approaches, across a spectrum of remote sensing scenarios. The findings consistently demonstrate that this strategy outperforms benchmark techniques with respect to relevance and descriptive quality. Potential uses of this technology are also investigated, including activities like environmental monitoring, disaster assessment, and land cover classification. These investigations demonstrate the adaptability and utility of the strategy across diverse fields. This research unveils a potential method for automatically generating textual descriptions from remote sensing images, aiding in the extraction of vital data for various geospatial applications. The suggested method makes strides in the field of remote sensing and has a great deal of promise for assisting decision-making in environmental and geographic studies.
