AB  - Humans communicate in spoken languages naturally and can easily understand or interpret what another person is saying. However, the hearing and speech impaired persons (HSI) cannot properly communicate orally, and it is very tough for listeners to interpret what is being said by them. To overcome this problem, sign language (SL) serves as a primary mode of communication for the HSI community. The limited societal awareness and fluency in sign language among SL users and non-SL users often restrict inclusive communication. To solve this issue, this study explores a deep learning (DL) system that is based on Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) using a random set of frequently used words from pre-recorded videos of Indian Sign Language (ISL) signs under the open-access INCLUDE dataset. A comprehensive preprocessing framework based on a BiLSTM model with estimation of its outcome, including frame extraction, landmark detection using MediaPipe Holistic, normalization, and data augmentation. The predictions based on the BiLSTM model have been compared with the results of other studies to determine whether it can attain higher accuracy and or remove their deficiency.
