AB  - Indonesia is a major player in the global mining sector, contributing 50.5% of the world's nickel supply. This causes significant land cover changes to nickel, bauxite, and silica sand downstreaming areas. This study explores the implications of geospatial data analysis on land cover changes in downstreaming areas by utilizing one of the Landsat-based platforms that combines CNN with specific monitoring of Indonesian mining downstream regions. Utilizing raster data from the Global Land Analysis & Discovery (GLAD) with a spatial resolution of 30 meters and over 85% classification accuracy, we analyzed land cover changes from 2000 to 2020. Findings confirm that 1%-3% of vegetation cover changes happened between the year 2000-2020. However, less than 1% of the deforested land has been revitalized into croplands or as forests, underscoring limited ecological recovery. To support this analysis, a web-based geodashboard, Petahilisisasi.id, using React, Leaflet, and FastAPI was developed. The findings underscore an important clause for sustainable downstreaming management and land revitalization policies in Indonesia.
