AB  - Environmental impact assessments have highlighted the impacts of mineral resource extraction activities on aquatic and marine ecosystems. These impacts are amplified in Pacific Island Countries and Territories (PICTs) including Fiji where fisheries contribute significantly to local economies and to village economies. In these contexts, there are constraints around the spatial and temporal coverage of the current environmental monitoring methods of local government regulators. As a result, Earth observation data has emerged as a potential resource to support environmental monitoring of Land Use Land Cover (LULC) changes as well as mineral extraction activities. The study investigates the use of both logistic regression and random forest machine learning classification approaches. These approaches rely upon high quality training data and annual cloud-free temporal composite satellite products to support the machine learning stages. The resulting products shed light on areas of mineral extraction in rivers and on land. Further testing in collaboration with the Mineral Resources Department of Fiji may be needed to improve the operationalization of this tool.
