AB  - Hyperspectral image (HSI) classification plays a critical role in remote sensing due to its ability to provide rich spectral information, with applications in environmental monitoring, agriculture, and other fields. While deep learning methods have made significant progress in extracting discriminative features from high-dimensional spectral data, challenges such as information loss and difficulty in capturing long-range dependencies persist. To address these issues, this paper proposes a novel spectral-spatial fusion MLP framework based on hypergraphs (HG-SSMLP). The framework models the underlying structure of HSI by constructing spectral and spatial hypergraphs, which are fused into a hybrid hypergraph for learning low-dimensional features. Furthermore, to mitigate the overfitting issues common in MLP-mixer architectures, a spatial shift mechanism is adopted rather than the SaMLP module for enhancing spatial communication through parameter-free operations while maintaining a local receptive field. Experimental results on two benchmark datasets demonstrate that HG-SSMLP outperforms some existing models, achieving higher classification accuracy.
