AB  - Surface reflectance and bidirectional reflectance distribution function are critical variables describing the surface reflectivity. This chapter first introduces the basic principles of atmospheric radiative transfer process and elaborates the effects of atmosphere on the remotely sensed signals, followed by the state-of-the-art approaches to remove the atmospheric effects in satellite images to derive surface reflectance. Cloud masking approaches are then described, including thresholding, machine-learning, and time-series algorithms to identify clouds and shadows. Various algorithms to correct topographic effects on surface reflectance are then introduced and inter-compared. With the clear-sky surface reflectance as input, bidirectional reflectance distribution function can be modeled in different forms and its key parameters can be retrieved. Some future research directions are mentioned at the end of this chapter, which include but are not limited to more validation activities, timely and accurate retrieval algorithms, and model development over complex surfaces.
