AB  - Marine and coastal environments are experiencing intensified accumulation of substances such as plastics, oil/chemical spills, dissolved gases, and nondegradable solid waste. Anthropogenic activities, including coastal dredging, seabed mining, overfishing, and transportation, also have a significant impact on marine ecosystems. Understanding the marine and coastal dynamics with pollutant linkages is essential for appropriate remedial measures to sustainably manage such environments. Contemporary monitoring and assessment include observatory attempts and sampling for elemental analysis to identify harmful substances. Usually, remedial measures regulate the conditions to acceptable levels of established standards. Time and resource-intensive such efforts were barely catering to dynamic environments until remote sensing and biomonitoring enabled swift and comprehensive tools. Satellite and UAV data at finer spatiotemporal resolutions and advanced machine learning algorithms have stretched the boundaries of real-time pollution monitoring and assessment. This chapter provides a systematic compilation of assessment approaches and mechanisms which have evolved over the recent past and retrieved from indexed academic databases.
