AB  - Lithological classification and identification is a significant research focus in the field of remote sensing geology. However, there are still many challenges due to the complexity of lithological compositions, the similar image characteristics of different lithology, and surface conditions, such as weathering and vegetation cover, which complicate the lithological classification process. Hyperspectral remote sensing, with its advantages of high spectral dimensional data, greatly enhances the capability for fine identification and discrimination of terrestrial features. Gaofen-5B satellite launched at Sept. 2021, carried the Advanced Hyperspectral Imager (AHSI) offers broad coverage and extremely high hyperspectral resolution, which promote the needs for large scale lithological mapping and mineral identification. We propose a method which combined superpixel segmentation with random forest (SLIC-RF) for lithological classification. Cluster pixels with similar features into a super-pixel region, then create a training sample set for random forest classification. Traditional and machine learning methods are used as competing methods, trained on GF-5B principal component analysis (PCA) results, and evaluated results according to training sets, overall accuracy, Kappa coefficient. The results indicate that the proposed method exhibits a significant effectiveness in the lithological classification of bedrock exposure areas. Compared with traditional methods, the proposed method based on superpixel segmentation and random forests demonstrates higher accuracy and robustness, enabling better discrimination of lithology with similar features and providing clearer lithological boundaries.
