AB  - Illegal mining is a severe threat to biodiversity hotspots like the Amazon. The early discovery of illicit airstrips, key indicators of such illegal operations, is critical for timely intervention by law enforcers. Traditional methods for this are often ineffective due to the vast area, human errors and environmental challenges. This paper presents an object detection framework for detecting these airstrips in satellite imagery using a multi-channel fusion approach combining dual-modality data from Sentinel-1 (SAR) and Sentinel-2 (optical) sensors. We introduce a technique where both radar and optical characteristics are obtained and concatenated into a joint 3-channel input to improve robustness under varied conditions. Utilizing a curated dataset of these coordinate-aligned images with precise Oriented Bounding Box (OBB) annotations, we implemented YOLOv8-OBB, a state-of-the-art object detection platform, which offers precise bounding of obliquely aligned targets. Our method demonstrates improved performance on key evaluation metrics, providing a robust, scalable tool to assist with the early detection and response to unauthorized land use, enhancing environmental monitoring efforts.
