AB  - Flash floods are among the most destructive natural disasters, demanding precise and efficient prediction methods. Conventional models often struggle with the dynamic and multi-scale nature of flood phenomena, leading to limited generalization and accuracy. To address these challenges, a novel approach, Dynamic Gold Rush-tuned Convolutional Block Attention Module with DeepLabv3 (DGR-CBAM-DLv3), is introduced. This method integrates spatial attention, semantic segmentation, and dynamic optimization to enhance predictive capabilities. The methodology involves preprocessing input datasets comprising rainfall patterns, digital elevation models, and satellite imagery using Min-Max normalization to ensure consistent scaling across features. High-dimensional feature extraction is performed using t-SNE, which captures complex patterns and reduces dimensionality while retaining critical flood-related information. CBAM enhances the model by prioritizing spatially and spectrally significant features, aiding the identification of flood-prone areas. DLv3+, equipped with an encoder-decoder structure, performs high-resolution segmentation to delineate risk zones accurately. The dynamic optimization mechanism, DGR, fine-tunes parameters adaptively, ensuring efficient convergence and robustness. This hybrid framework captures both local and global flood indicators, achieving superior segmentation and predictive performance. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements compared to baseline methods in terms of sensitivity (89.74%), specificity (92.31%), accuracy (92.85%) and reduced RMSE (0.21), validating the approach's effectiveness in mapping and predicting flash flood risks. The proposed system advances real-time flood risk management by providing high-resolution, interpretable outputs, enabling better decision-making. This methodology underscores the potential of combining attention mechanisms, segmentation models, and dynamic optimization for tackling complex hydrological challenges.
