AB  - Drone-based water body extraction faces unique challenges due to the low-altitude perspective and complex occlusion scenarios involving vegetation, clouds, and man-made structures that obscure water boundaries in drone imagery. Traditional deep learning methods rely solely on visual features and often fail to accurately segment water bodies in heavily occluded regions captured by drones, leading to incomplete extraction results. To address this limitation, this paper proposes a novel text-vision joint model specifically designed for drone-based water body extraction that leverages descriptive text containing explicit occlusion information to guide accurate water body extraction. The proposed dual-branch encoder-decoder architecture integrates visual features from drone imagery with textual descriptions through cross-scale interaction and frequency-enhanced processing mechanisms. Ablation studies and feature visualization analysis validate the effectiveness of each architectural component. Extensive experiments on the VDD dataset demonstrate that our method achieves 94.94% IoU and 97.96% F1-score, significantly outperforming state-of-the-art approaches with superior generalization capability across diverse geographical regions and environmental conditions typical of drone-based monitoring scenarios.
