AB  - Rare Earth Elements (REE) and Critical Minerals (CM) are indispensable to modern industry, powering everything from strategic defense systems to semiconductors and batteries. Yet securing their supply chains remains a global challenge, particularly in conflict-affected regions such as the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), where vast mineral wealth is entangled with armed conflict, illicit trade, and geopolitical competition. This research explores how space-based technologies—including remote sensing, hyperspectral imaging, synthetic aperture radar (SAR), thermal infrared data, and radio frequency (RF) monitoring—can transform mineral exploration and supply chain oversight in contested areas. By leveraging commercial and government satellite assets, this study demonstrates how persistent, wide-area surveillance can detect mineral-rich zones, monitor mining operations, and track illicit extraction even in remote and insecure regions. Multi-temporal analysis of satellite imagery reveals patterns of mining expansion, deforestation linked to artisanal and illegal extraction, and shifts in transport routes used for mineral smuggling. Complementing these physical insights, satellite-enabled communications analysis and AI-driven geospatial intelligence provide visibility into the networks, logistics, and financial flows that sustain illicit mineral trade. At the core of this approach is OREBIT, a next-generation platform that fuses multi-source satellite data with AI analytics, financial transaction monitoring, and open-source intelligence (OSINT) to deliver real-time, user-friendly situational awareness. By applying OREBIT to the DRC’s contested mining zones, this research illustrates how space-based intelligence can mitigate illicit mineral exploitation, strengthen global supply chain security, and create pathways for more ethical and sustainable resource access. Moreover, OREBIT is inherently scalable—its integration of space-derived data and AI analytics can be extended to other contested geographies such as the Sahel, Middle East, or Arctic, offering a transformative capability for securing critical resources in volatile environments worldwide.
