AB  - Land surface emissivity represents the ability of the land surface to emit thermal radiance, which is widely required in the fields of land surface temperature (LST) retrieval, mineral mapping, weather forecasting, etc. The current land surface emissivity estimation based on remote sensing includes semiempirical methods, multiband temperature emissivity separation methods, and physics-based methods (PBMs). Semiempirical methods are usually used for emissivity estimation based on Landsat thermal infrared (TIR) data due to the limitation of the number of TIR bands and imaging modalities of Landsat. However, these methods rely on land cover classifications or other external remote sensing data sources, which may introduce additional errors. In this study, for Landsat data, a pixel-mixed spectral simulation dataset containing bare soil/rocks, sparse vegetation, and dense vegetation is established by combining the ASTER/ECOSTRESS spectral library and Landsat-band spectral response function, and a regression model based on the CatBoost model considering data noise is adopted to obtain emissivity estimation results with an RMSE of 0.007. The emissivity estimation results are applied to LST retrieval and validated using ground measurements at the SURFRAD sites, achieving the results with similar accuracy to the existing Landsat LST products (2.920 and 2.976 K). The method proposed in this study gets rid of the dependence on external data and generates emissivity directly from Landsat multiband reflectance products, which provides superior dynamics and completeness compared to existing Landsat emissivity products. Future improvements in terms of angular effect correction, urban and water emissivity optimization, and under-cloud reconstruction will be considered to further enhance the performance of emissivity estimation.
