AB  - In multimodal remote sensing image classification, due to the difficulty in capturing the spectral-spatial distributions of heterogeneous data and relationships between samples, the case will lead to a decrease in the classification ability of model. To address this issue, we propose a dual-branch diffusion model-based cross attention fusion classification model (DMCA). This model consists of two parts. In the feature extraction section, diffusion models are used to obtain global features of different modalities, effectively mining fine-grained features of heterogeneous data and relationships between samples. In feature fusion classification part, the model uses three modules, including a 2D CNN module, a position feature embedding (PFE) module, and transformer cross attention fusion modules, which can deeply extract diffusion features and promote feature interaction fusion. Experiments on the Houston dataset demonstrate that our method outperforms other state-of-the-art methods.
