AB  - Salt lakes are rich in mineral resources and hold significant economic and ecological value. Qarhan Salt Lake, the largest salt lake in China, is rich in minerals such as magnesium and lithium. Dynamic monitoring of the salt lake region’s layout through remote sensing or UAV technologies is crucial for salt production in China. Remote sensing methods are particularly notable for their wide coverage, high efficiency, and ability to monitor over the long term. Accurate segmentation of the salt lake and different salt field regions is essential for monitoring salt production. However, due to the large scale, high spatial heterogeneity, and dense arrangement of salt lakes and salt fields, current semantic segmentation methods perform poorly in this scenario, struggling to define boundaries and identify complete targets. Additionally, traditional deep learning networks have limited generalization abilities and struggle to adapt to changes in salt field layouts caused by seasonal variations or human activities. In contrast, SAM (Segment Anything Model) offers powerful general segmentation capabilities, but it performs poorly on domain-specific images and lacks sufficient semantic information. To address this, we propose an object-level segmentation framework. We first introduce a high-frequency feature extraction module to improve SAM’s segmentation accuracy, then treat the segmentation results as independent objects, using salinity index and temporal salinity correlation entropy to extract spatial and temporal features for precise classification. Experimental evaluation using Sentinel-2 and GF-06 imagery shows that, compared to mainstream methods, our approach improves mIoU and mDice by 1.74% and 1.10%, respectively, providing a robust solution for large-scale segmentation tasks.
