AB  - With the development of industry and the exploitation of mineral resources, a large amount of heavy metal elements will leak into the natural environment, seriously affecting the health of the ecological environment and becoming an important global problem. Excessive heavy metal content not only stresses crop growth but also affects food production and people's health. Traditional manual sampling and inspection methods are costly and inefficient. This study aims to fully explore the potential of ZY-1 02D hyperspectral imaging in monitoring heavy metal pollution in crops over large areas. A mining area in Laiyuan County, Hebei Province was selected as the research area. First, we collected leaves from the corn crop canopy in the field, extracted leaf spectra, and tested the heavy metal content in the leaves. Harmonic analysis (HA) was used for spectral reconstruction to remove complex environmental noise from the leaf spectra. HA-SVM and HA-RF classifiers were constructed to extract corn crop information from ZY-1 02D hyperspectral remote sensing imagery in the study area. Secondly, Pearson correlation analysis (Cor) and successive projections algorithm (SPA) are used to reduce the dimensionality of the spectral data. Finally, random forest (RF), support vector machine (SVM), Lasso regression analysis, XGBoost, and AdaBoost models were used to estimate the heavy metal content in corn crop leaves, evaluate and compare them, and determine the best estimation model. The results show that when the original spectrum modeling accuracy is poor, HA can help improve the relationship between the spectrum and the heavy metal content, especially the accuracy of the Cr, Ni, Zn, As, and Pb content estimation model is significantly higher than that of the model established with the original spectrum. Finally, the optimal estimation model was applied to ZY-1 02D hyperspectral remote sensing images to map the contents of heavy metals Cu, Pb, Cr, Ni, Zn, As, Cd, and Hg in corn crops in the study area. The results confirmed the great potential of combining ZY-1 02D hyperspectral imaging with machine learning algorithms for monitoring heavy metal pollution in crops. This study provides guarantee and technical support for the inversion monitoring of heavy metal pollution in large-scale food crops.
